.TH EJSON-ENCRYPT 1 "January 2026" "ejson" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ejson-encrypt \- encrypt secrets in EJSON, ETOML, or EYAML files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ejson encrypt
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ejson encrypt
encrypts string values in one or more secrets files using public-key
cryptography. The files are modified in place.
.PP
The encryption uses the public key embedded in each file's
.B _public_key
field. An ephemeral keypair is generated for each encryption session to
provide forward secrecy.
.PP
Already-encrypted values (those starting with
.BR "EJ[" )
are left unchanged, making it safe to run
.B ejson encrypt
multiple times on the same file. This allows you to add new secrets to an
existing file and encrypt only the new values.
.SH OPTIONS
This command takes no options. File format is auto-detected based on
file extension.
.SH FILE FORMAT
Format detection is automatic based on file extension:
.TP
.B JSON
Extensions:
.IR .ejson ", " .json
.TP
.B TOML
Extensions:
.IR .etoml ", " .toml
.TP
.B YAML
Extensions:
.IR .eyaml ", " .eyml ", " .yaml ", " .yml
.SH ENCRYPTION RULES
The following rules apply to all supported formats:
.IP 1. 4
.B Public key required
\- The file must have a top-level
.B _public_key
field containing a 64-character hex-encoded public key.
.IP 2. 4
.B Strings are encrypted
\- All string values are encrypted by default.
.IP 3. 4
.B Other types preserved
\- Numbers, booleans, nulls, and dates remain as plaintext.
.IP 4. 4
.B Underscore prefix skips encryption
\- Keys starting with
.B _
(underscore) protect their immediate string value from encryption.
.IP 5. 4
.B Underscores don't propagate
\- Nested values under an underscore-prefixed key are still encrypted
unless they also have underscore prefixes.
.IP 6. 4
.B Arrays work element-by-element
\- Each string element in an array is encrypted individually.
.IP 7. 4
.B Already encrypted values unchanged
\- Values starting with
.B EJ[
are not re-encrypted.
.SH ENCRYPTED VALUE FORMAT
Encrypted values have the format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
EJ[1:<nonce>:<ciphertext>]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Where:
.IP \(bu 2
.B 1
\- Version identifier
.IP \(bu 2
.B <nonce>
\- Base64-encoded encryption nonce
.IP \(bu 2
.B <ciphertext>
\- Base64-encoded encrypted data
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Encrypt a single file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson encrypt secrets.ejson
Wrote 348 bytes to secrets.ejson.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Encrypt multiple files:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson encrypt secrets.ejson production.ejson staging.ejson
Wrote 348 bytes to secrets.ejson.
Wrote 512 bytes to production.ejson.
Wrote 256 bytes to staging.ejson.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Encrypt all secrets files in a directory:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson encrypt config/*.ejson
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B JSON example
\- Before encryption:
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "_database_username": "admin",
  "database_password": "supersecret123"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
After encryption:
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "_database_username": "admin",
  "database_password": "EJ[1:WGj2t4znULHT1IRveMEdvvNXqZzNBNMsJ5iZVy6Dvxs=:kA6ekF8ViYR5ZLeSmMXWsdLfWr7wn9qS:fcHQtdt6nqcNOXa97/M278RX6w==]"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B TOML example
\- Before encryption:
.RS
.nf
_public_key = "63ccf05a..."

_database_username = "admin"       # Not encrypted
database_password = "supersecret"  # Will be encrypted

[api]
secret_key = "api-secret-key"      # Will be encrypted
_endpoint = "https://api.example.com"  # Not encrypted
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B YAML example
\- Before encryption:
.RS
.nf
_public_key: "63ccf05a..."

_database_username: "admin"        # Not encrypted
database_password: "supersecret"   # Will be encrypted

api:
  secret_key: "api-secret-key"     # Will be encrypted
  _endpoint: "https://api.example.com"  # Not encrypted

allowed_hosts:                     # Each element encrypted
  - "host1.example.com"
  - "host2.example.com"
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. All files were encrypted.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (file not found, invalid format, missing public key,
file too large, etc.).
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Encryption uses NaCl Box (Curve25519 + Salsa20 + Poly1305-AES) for
authenticated public-key encryption.
.IP \(bu 2
An ephemeral keypair is generated for each encryption session, providing
forward secrecy. Even if the document's private key is later compromised,
past encrypted values cannot be decrypted without the ephemeral key.
.IP \(bu 2
File operations use exclusive locking to prevent race conditions when
multiple processes attempt to encrypt the same file.
.IP \(bu 2
Maximum file size is limited to 10 MB to prevent denial of service through
memory exhaustion.
.IP \(bu 2
Path traversal attacks are prevented through validation (no device files,
FIFOs, or sockets are allowed).
.IP \(bu 2
The file's original permissions are preserved after encryption.
.IP \(bu 2
Encrypted files are safe to commit to version control. Only the public key
is needed for encryption; the private key is only needed for decryption.
.SH TIPS
.IP \(bu 2
Use a
.BR pre-commit
hook to automatically encrypt secrets before committing. See the ejson-rs
repository for a pre-commit hook configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
Use meaningful underscore-prefixed keys for non-sensitive metadata like
comments or environment names (e.g.,
.IR _comment ", " _environment ).
.IP \(bu 2
Run
.B ejson encrypt
after adding new secrets to ensure they are encrypted before committing.
.IP \(bu 2
Use
.B git diff
to verify that only intended changes were made before committing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ejson (1),
.BR ejson (5),
.BR ejson\-keygen (1),
.BR ejson\-decrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-env (1)
